window.BOOK_SEARCH = [{"title": "What a Quantum Model Is — Exercise 1", "chapter": "What a Quantum Model Is", "url": "01-quantum-models.html#exercise-1", "text": "Which part of a learning task does a quantum circuit replace? The hypothesis class. The distribution and the loss are properties of the task; the circuit only fixes which functions are available and how they are computed."}, {"title": "What a Quantum Model Is — Exercise 2", "chapter": "What a Quantum Model Is", "url": "01-quantum-models.html#exercise-2", "text": "Four points have labels (0,0,0,1). What is the empirical risk of the best constant classifier under zero–one loss? Predicting 0 is wrong once, so the risk is 1/4 = 0.25. Predicting 1 is wrong three times. A constant classifier on a balanced set gives 0.5."}, {"title": "What a Quantum Model Is — Exercise 3", "chapter": "What a Quantum Model Is", "url": "01-quantum-models.html#exercise-3", "text": "A 20-qubit circuit has 2^20 amplitudes, so it has 2^20 parameters and is automatically more expressive than a 20-parameter classical model. Correct this. No. A circuit with p angles is a p-dimensional family of functions, exactly like a classical model with p weights. The state space is large, but the model explores only a p-dimensional slice of it. Expressivity is a property of that slice, not of the Hilbert-space dimension."}, {"title": "What a Quantum Model Is — Exercise 4", "chapter": "What a Quantum Model Is", "url": "01-quantum-models.html#exercise-4", "text": "For h(x)=cos(θ+x) with θ=π/4, compute h(0) and h(π/2). h(0)=cos(π/4)≈0.7071 and h(π/2)=cos(3π/4)≈−0.7071. Both values lie in the reachable range of the single-frequency model, which is a degree-one trigonometric polynomial."}, {"title": "What a Quantum Model Is — Exercise 5", "chapter": "What a Quantum Model Is", "url": "01-quantum-models.html#exercise-5", "text": "What fixes the set of frequencies a Pauli-encoded circuit can represent? The eigenvalues of the encoding generators, together with the number of times the encoding is applied. With L Pauli-generated layers and integer eigenvalues, the accessible frequencies are the integers from −L to L in each feature."}, {"title": "What a Quantum Model Is — Exercise 6", "chapter": "What a Quantum Model Is", "url": "01-quantum-models.html#exercise-6", "text": "A training loss that does not decrease means the optimiser has converged. Correct this. No. A flat loss means the gradient is (near) zero, which happens at minima, maxima, and saddles alike, and also at initialisation points that sit exactly on a stationary value. The statement to check is where the trajectory started and whether the value is one you would accept."}, {"title": "What a Quantum Model Is — Exercise 7", "chapter": "What a Quantum Model Is", "url": "01-quantum-models.html#exercise-7", "text": "For L(θ)=1−cos θ with gradient descent started at θ=π, describe the trajectory and classify the point. The gradient sin π = 0, so the trajectory never moves and L stays at 2, the maximum of the loss. The model could achieve L=0 at θ=0, so this is an initialisation failure, not an expressivity limit."}, {"title": "What a Quantum Model Is — Exercise 8", "chapter": "What a Quantum Model Is", "url": "01-quantum-models.html#exercise-8", "text": "A quantum kernel experiment uses N=200 points and S=1000 shots per entry. Count circuit executions for the Gram matrix, and the time at 1 microsecond per shot. The matrix has 200² = 40,000 entries, each needing 1000 shots, so 4×10^7 shots, about 40 seconds at 1 microsecond each. Training and testing add more evaluations on top of this."}, {"title": "What a Quantum Model Is — Exercise 9", "chapter": "What a Quantum Model Is", "url": "01-quantum-models.html#exercise-9", "text": "Rewrite 'quantum kernels outperform classical kernels on our dataset' as a claim that could be checked. A checkable version names the dataset and split, the classical baseline and how it was tuned, the metric, the uncertainty over splits, and the cost budget at which both were compared. It also states whether the claim is an existence separation, a runtime speedup, or a practical advantage."}, {"title": "What a Quantum Model Is — Exercise 10", "chapter": "What a Quantum Model Is", "url": "01-quantum-models.html#exercise-10", "text": "A paper reports 95% test accuracy for a 10-qubit kernel and 80% for an RBF kernel, with the feature map chosen by inspecting test accuracy. Name the two failures and the repair. Test leakage (the map was selected using test labels) and a possibly weak baseline. The repair is to select the map and its hyperparameters on training folds only, tune the classical baseline under the same protocol, and report both with the spread over splits."}, {"title": "Learning tasks and hypothesis classes", "chapter": "What a Quantum Model Is", "url": "01-quantum-models.html#section-1-1", "text": "1.1 Learning tasks and hypothesis classes Before asking whether a quantum model is better, we need a precise account of what it is being compared with. This section fixes the vocabulary: a task, a class, and a training rule are three different objects, and only the first two define what can be learned in principle. 1.1.1 State the task before the circuit A supervised learning task is a triple: a distribution \\(D\\) on \\(\\mathcal X\\times\\mathcal Y\\), a hypothesis class \\(\\mathcal H=\\{h_\\theta\\}\\), and a loss \\(\\ell\\). The quantity of interest is the risk \\[R(\\theta)=\\mathbb E_{(x,y)\\sim D}\\,\\ell\\bigl(h_\\theta(x),y\\bigr),\\] which nobody can compute exactly, because \\(D\\) is unknown. What is computable is the empirical risk on a sample \\(S=\\{(x_i,y_i)\\}_{i=1}^N\\), \\[\\widehat R_S(\\theta)=\\frac1N\\sum_{i=1}^N\\ell\\bigl(h_\\theta(x_i),y_i\\bigr),\\] and the object of interest is the gap between the two. A learning algorithm succeeds if it returns \\(\\theta\\) whose risk is close to the best achievable in \\(\\mathcal H\\), using only \\(S\\). Notice what this definition does not mention: circuits, qubits, or gates. A quantum model enters only when we specify \\(\\mathcal H\\): the hypothesis is a function computed by preparing a state that depends on \\(x\\) and \\(\\theta\\), then measuring. The task fixes the yardstick; the model chooses how the function is computed. Writing the task first is what prevents the common slide from \"our circuit fits the training data\" to \"we have learned the task\". Worked example Four labelled points are given: \\((0,0),(1,1),(2,0),(3,1)\\). Compute the empirical risk of the best constant classifier under zero–one loss, and of the classifier \\(h(x)=\\mathbb 1[x\\ge 1.5]\\). Solution and reasoning Two points have label 0 and two have label 1, so any constant predictor is wrong on exactly two points: \\(\\widehat R=2/4=0.5\\). The threshold rule predicts 0 on the first two and 1 on the last two, so \\(\\widehat R=0\\). The example is trivial, but it already shows why a single number is not evidence: the threshold rule can also achieve zero empirical risk on a dataset engineered to make it look perfect, and we still know nothing about \\(R\\). Only a statement about the distribution—or a bound that holds uniformly over \\(\\mathcal H\\)—converts empirical success into a claim about learning. 1.1.2 Hypothesis classes and what quantum adds A quantum hypothesis class is fixed by three choices: an encoding \\(x\\mapsto\\rho(x)\\), a parameterised circuit \\(W(\\theta)\\), and a measurement \\(M\\). The hypothesis is \\[h_\\theta(x)=\\operatorname{tr}\\!\\bigl[W(\\theta)\\rho(x)W(\\theta)^{\\dagger}M\\bigr],\\] a real number in the range of \\(M\\). For binary classification, a threshold turns it into a label; for regression it is the prediction. Everything the model can do is contained in this triple, so any two models with the same triple are the same model—whatever their circuit diagrams look like. What does quantum mechanics add to the hypothesis class? Not \"more parameters\": a circuit with \\(p\\) angles is a \\(p\\)-dimensional family, exactly like a classical model with \\(p\\) weights. What changes is the shape of that family inside function space. Three mechanisms matter, and only the third is specifically quantum. Nonlinearity through amplitudes. Data enters as rotation angles or amplitudes, so the map from \\(x\\) to features is nonlinear in a way that is not a fixed feature map chosen by hand. Interference. Amplitudes add before probabilities are formed, so different \"paths\" through a circuit can cancel. This is what allows a compact circuit to represent functions with many Fourier components. Entanglement. Intermediate states can carry correlations between features that no product-state model represents, which is the resource that makes some of these circuits hard to simulate classically. One concrete restriction is worth stating early, because it disciplines the intuition. When the encoding is a product of single-qubit rotations generated by Pauli operators and the observable is fixed, the model is a trigonometric polynomial in the data: a finite Fourier series whose frequencies are set by the encoding's generator eigenvalues. This is a theorem with hypotheses, not a slogan, and we return to it in Chapter 2. Worked example Take a single qubit, encoding \\(R_z(x)=\\exp(-ixZ/2)\\), a parameter \\(\\theta\\), and the model \\(h_\\theta(x)=\\langle 0|R_z(\\theta)R_z(x)\\,Z\\,R_z(x)^{\\dagger}R_z(\\theta)^{\\dagger}|0\\rangle\\). Write \\(h_\\theta(x)\\) in closed form and identify its Fourier structure. Solution and reasoning The two rotations combine into \\(R_z(\\theta+x)\\), so \\(h=\\langle 0|R_z(\\theta+x)ZR_z(\\theta+x)^{\\dagger}|0\\rangle=\\cos(\\theta+x)\\). Expanding gives \\(h_\\theta(x)=\\cos\\theta\\cos x-\\sin\\theta\\sin x\\), a degree-one trigonometric polynomial whose coefficients are the trainable parameters. Two parameters, two frequencies, and no interference: a single rotation cannot produce a richer function however it is trained. Repeating the encoding block and interleaving entanglers is what adds frequencies—the subject of Chapter 2. 1.1.3 Separate the model from the training rule A model is a class plus a loss. A training rule is a procedure that searches the class. The same model trained two ways can produce very different results, and a statement about one is not a statement about the other. It is useful to keep three questions apart, because each has its own kind of evidence: The three claims a quantum machine learning paper can make, and what would support each. Claim Question Evidence that supports it Expressivity Which functions are in \\(\\mathcal H\\)? A structural theorem, a Fourier or polynomial characterisation, or a counting argument Trainability Can a stated algorithm find good parameters? A convergence guarantee, a gradient-variance bound, or a controlled numerical study Generalisation Does low empirical risk imply low risk? A bound that holds uniformly over \\(\\mathcal H\\), or a pre-registered test comparison Confusing these is the most common error in the field. A circuit whose class contains every function on \\(n\\) bits is expressive and useless if the optimiser cannot find the right member; a circuit that trains reliably is unremarkable if its class is a subset of linear models; and a model that generalises on one dataset says nothing about the class at large. Worked example Consider the one-parameter loss \\(L(\\theta)=1-\\cos\\theta\\). Show that gradient descent initialised at \\(\\theta_0=\\pi\\) never moves, and classify that stationary point. Solution and reasoning The gradient is \\(\\sin\\theta\\), which vanishes at \\(\\theta=\\pi\\). Gradient descent therefore stays at \\(\\theta=\\pi\\) forever, where \\(L=2\\)—the maximum of the loss, not a minimum. The point is not that the model is inexpressive (it attains \\(L=0\\) at \\(\\theta=0\\)) nor that the optimiser is broken in general; it is that a trainability claim needs an argument about where the trajectory starts and how it moves. Reporting \"the loss did not decrease\" would be a statement about initialisation, not about the model. The pipeline of a quantum machine learning model Classical data x is encoded into a quantum state, transformed by a parameterised circuit, and measured to produce a prediction. A loss compares the prediction with the label, and a classical optimiser updates the parameters and repeats. data x classical encoding ρ(x) W(θ) trained measure M prediction loss ℓ optimiser update θ One training step. The quantum device evaluates the model; the loss and the parameter update are classical. Every cost claim must therefore count both sides of this loop: state preparation, circuit execution with repeated shots, and the classical optimisation that drives it."}, {"title": "Where an advantage could come from", "chapter": "What a Quantum Model Is", "url": "01-quantum-models.html#section-1-2", "text": "1.2 Where an advantage could come from If a quantum model is a classical function computed on a quantum device, an advantage has to be explained. This section catalogues the legitimate sources, and insists on a ledger that includes the cost of loading the data. 1.2.1 Data that is hard to load and hard to simulate Three mechanisms can, in principle, give a quantum learner something a classical learner cannot cheaply reproduce. Quantum data. If the data are themselves quantum states—a molecule's ground state, a scattering event, a state produced by an experiment—then a learner that consumes states directly avoids a tomographic bottleneck. This is the cleanest case, because no classical vector has to be loaded: the sample is the state. Hard-to-evaluate models. Some circuits produce feature vectors whose inner products are believed hard to estimate classically. If a learning problem aligns with that structure, evaluating the model—not merely storing its parameters—can be the expensive step. Chapter 3 makes this precise with kernels. Hard-to-represent functions. A compact circuit can represent functions with exponentially many Fourier components. Whether such a function is learnable from polynomially many examples is a separate question, and often the answer is no. The counterweight is loading. Preparing an arbitrary \\(n\\)-qubit state requires exponentially many gates in general, so a classical dataset of \\(2^n\\) numbers cannot be poured into amplitudes for free. Either the algorithm assumes a data structure that supplies amplitudes on demand—an assumption about hardware that does not currently exist at scale—or the loading cost must be counted in the ledger. Any claim that omits this step is measuring the wrong thing. Worked example A dataset has \\(2^{10}=1024\\) features per point. Compare the cost of amplitude encoding one point exactly with the cost of ten rotation angles. Solution and reasoning Amplitude encoding needs ten qubits and, in the worst case, on the order of \\(2^{10}=1024\\) two-qubit gates to prepare an arbitrary normalised vector; angle encoding needs ten rotation gates, one per qubit. The two encodings are therefore three orders of magnitude apart at this size, and the gap doubles with every additional qubit. The comparison is not an argument against amplitude encoding—it is an argument that its cost must appear in the ledger, and that a claim of advantage must show the model's benefit exceeds this price. 1.2.2 A three-way ledger: data, model, algorithm Every fair comparison can be written as a sum of three costs: \\[\\text{total}=\\underbrace{C_{\\text{load}}}_{\\text{prepare }\\rho(x)}+\\underbrace{N_{\\text{eval}}\\cdot C_{\\text{eval}}}_{\\text{run the circuit}}+\\underbrace{C_{\\text{opt}}}_{\\text{update parameters}},\\] where \\(N_{\\text{eval}}\\) counts circuit executions including shots. A quantum advantage requires this total to be smaller than the best classical alternative—not merely that one term is smaller. Where the cost sits for a classical kernel method and a quantum kernel method on the same dataset of \\(N\\) points in \\(d\\) dimensions. Stage Classical kernel Quantum kernel Load one example \\(O(d)\\) memory access State preparation, up to \\(O(2^{n})\\) gates, unless an oracle is assumed One kernel entry \\(O(d)\\) arithmetic One circuit execution, repeated for shots Full Gram matrix \\(O(N^2d)\\) \\(O(N^2)\\) circuits, each with \\(S\\) shots Training Convex in the dual Convex in the dual, once entries are known The table already explains a persistent empirical observation: at modest \\(N\\), the classical kernel is cheap because its per-entry cost is a few dozen floating-point operations, while the quantum kernel requires a full circuit execution per entry. Making the quantum side competitive requires either an entry cost that is classically prohibitive or a problem where the classical kernel needs a feature dimension far larger than \\(d\\). Worked example Estimate the circuit executions needed for a quantum kernel on \\(N=1000\\) training points with \\(S=1000\\) shots per entry, and the wall-clock time at one microsecond per shot. Solution and reasoning The Gram matrix has \\(N^2=10^{6}\\) entries, and each needs \\(S=10^{3}\\) shots, so \\(10^{9}\\) shots in total. At \\(1\\,\\mu s\\) each this is \\(10^{3}\\) seconds—about seventeen minutes—for a single kernel matrix, before any training or testing. A classical kernel on the same 1000 points with \\(d=100\\) takes roughly \\(10^{6}\\times10^{2}=10^{8}\\) arithmetic operations, well under a second. The quantum route must therefore win on something other than per-entry constant factors, which is why the interesting arguments are asymptotic and structural rather than empirical at small scale. 1.2.3 Rule out the classical baseline first The strongest empirical QML papers of the last few years share a habit: they compute a strong classical baseline before claiming anything. The pattern is familiar from other fields, but QML has two extra hazards. The first is that the quantum model is often evaluated on data that was constructed to be hard for a specific classical model—say, data whose labels depend on a parity that a linear classifier cannot express. A gradient-boosted tree or an RBF kernel may solve it easily, so the baseline must be chosen to be strong, not convenient. The second is the \"quantum-inspired\" baseline. A classical algorithm derived from the same mathematics—random features, tensor networks, or a classical simulation of the circuit—can often reproduce the quantum model's predictions at a fraction of the cost. If it can, the experiment has demonstrated a simulation, not an advantage. A useful discipline is to write the comparison before running anything: which classical model, with which hyperparameters, on which data split, at what budget. Deciding afterwards which baseline to report is how weak comparisons survive review."}, {"title": "Claims, assumptions, and honest comparisons", "chapter": "What a Quantum Model Is", "url": "01-quantum-models.html#section-1-3", "text": "1.3 Claims, assumptions, and honest comparisons An advantage claim is a mathematical statement with quantifiers, and it can be checked like any other. This section turns the rhetoric into a template, lists the failure modes that recur, and states what this course does and does not attempt. 1.3.1 Read an advantage claim as a conditional A precise claim has the form \\[\\exists\\,\\text{task }(D,\\mathcal H,\\ell)\\ \\ \\forall\\,\\text{classical algorithms } A\\ \\ \\exists n:\\ \\ \\text{cost}_Q(n)<\\text{cost}_A(n),\\] with the cost model, the access to data, and the accuracy target all named. Different quantifier orders give very different statements, and the differences are not pedantry: Existence separation. Some task separates the two models. Says nothing about the task you have. Runtime speedup. For one task, the quantum algorithm is asymptotically faster at a stated accuracy. Says nothing about time to solution at realistic sizes. Practical advantage. On the data and hardware available, the quantum method wins under a pre-registered protocol. This is the only claim with direct empirical content, and it is also the most fragile. Reading a paper, the useful question is which of these three is being claimed, and whether the evidence matches. A theorem establishes the first; a complexity analysis establishes the second; only a controlled experiment with a strong baseline establishes the third. Worked example Rewrite this sentence precisely: \"Quantum kernels outperform classical kernels on our dataset.\" Solution and reasoning A defensible version is: \"On dataset \\(D\\) with a fixed train/test split, a support vector machine using the estimated quantum kernel \\(K_Q\\) achieved test accuracy \\(a_Q\\), compared with \\(a_C\\) for an RBF kernel tuned by cross-validation on the training folds; the difference is \\(a_Q-a_C\\) with a bootstrap interval over splits, at equal total shot and wall-clock budget.\" The rewrite adds the dataset, the baseline and its tuning rule, the metric, the uncertainty, and the cost model. Without those five items the original sentence cannot be true or false—only persuasive. 1.3.2 Common failure modes The same mistakes recur across the literature, and each has a cheap diagnostic. Recurring failure modes, and the check that catches each one. Failure What it looks like Diagnostic Simulator advantage Noise-free state-vector results at 20+ qubits Run the same circuit with a classical simulator and compare cost Barren plateau Loss flat from the first iteration Plot gradient variance against qubit count and depth Weak baseline Only linear models are compared Add a tuned RBF kernel or gradient boosting Test leakage Encoding or kernel chosen after seeing test labels Fix the model before touching the test split Unequal budgets Quantum gets more shots than the classical model gets time Report both at matched wall-clock Surrogate metric Kernel-target alignment instead of accuracy Report the task metric as well None of these is exotic. Each is a natural consequence of working with expensive hardware, small datasets, and a strong incentive to report the best number. Chapter 7 returns to them with quantitative tools. Worked example A paper reports 96% test accuracy with a 12-qubit kernel and 78% with an RBF kernel. It says the quantum kernel was chosen after inspecting kernel-target alignment on the full dataset. Which failure does this describe, and what would repair it? Solution and reasoning This is test leakage combined with a weak baseline. Selecting the kernel using alignment computed on all the data—including the test points—optimistically biases the reported accuracy, because the choice has already seen the labels it is later scored against. The repair is procedural: choose the feature map and its hyperparameters using only training folds, tune the RBF baseline with the same protocol, and report both with the spread over splits. Note that the repair may well remove the apparent advantage; that is the point of doing it. 1.3.3 What this course will and will not prove It is worth being explicit about scope, because QML writing often inflates it. This course will derive the structure of quantum models: how encodings determine the Fourier spectrum, why kernels concentrate, where gradients vanish, and what uniform-convergence bounds do and do not say. It will give you the arithmetic to check a claim yourself, and a laboratory in which to reproduce every numerical example with the Python standard library. This course will not prove a separation between quantum and classical learners, certify an advantage on real data, or survey the hardware literature. Those are research results with their own hypotheses; the course's job is to make you able to read them. Four gates an advantage claim must pass A claim passes through expressivity, then trainability, then generalisation, and only then can it support an advantage statement. A failure at any gate stops the claim. expressivity is it in ℋ? trainability can we find it? generality does it transfer? advantage over a baseline a failure at any gate stops the claim, whatever the others show The order matters. Expressivity without trainability is a statement about a class nobody can search; trainability without generality is a statement about one dataset; and none of the three, alone, is an advantage—the last gate requires a comparison with a strong classical method at a stated budget. The remaining chapters follow this structure. Chapter 2 asks what an encoding does to the model's frequency content. Chapter 3 develops kernels and their quantum versions. Chapters 4 and 5 treat variational models and their gradients. Chapter 6 is devoted to trainability failures. Chapter 7 takes up generalisation and evaluation. Chapter 8 examines the classical algorithms that compete with quantum models. Chapter 9 is the audit project."}, {"title": "Encoding Data into Quantum States — Exercise 1", "chapter": "Encoding Data into Quantum States", "url": "02-data-encoding.html#exercise-1", "text": "How many qubits and gates does basis encoding of a 4-bit string need? Four qubits and one X gate per 1 in the string: for 1011, three X gates."}, {"title": "Encoding Data into Quantum States — Exercise 2", "chapter": "Encoding Data into Quantum States", "url": "02-data-encoding.html#exercise-2", "text": "Write the amplitude-encoded state for x=(1,1,1,1) and give a circuit that prepares it. The state is (1,1,1,1)/2, which is H⊗H|00⟩. Two Hadamard gates prepare it, and no data-dependent angles are needed."}, {"title": "Encoding Data into Quantum States — Exercise 3", "chapter": "Encoding Data into Quantum States", "url": "02-data-encoding.html#exercise-3", "text": "How many real parameters does a general 5-qubit state have, and what does that imply for preparation? 2^6 − 2 = 62 parameters. Each gate supplies a constant number, so preparing an arbitrary 5-qubit state needs on the order of 60 gates; for n qubits the count scales as 2^n."}, {"title": "Encoding Data into Quantum States — Exercise 4", "chapter": "Encoding Data into Quantum States", "url": "02-data-encoding.html#exercise-4", "text": "For the product-state angle encoding, compute K between x=(π/2, 0) and x'=(0, 0). cos(π/4)·cos(0) = 0.7071, so K = 0.5. The second feature is identical in both points and contributes a factor of one."}, {"title": "Encoding Data into Quantum States — Exercise 5", "chapter": "Encoding Data into Quantum States", "url": "02-data-encoding.html#exercise-5", "text": "Angle encoding maps every real feature value to a distinct quantum state, so it loses no information. Correct this. No. R_y(α+2π) = −R_y(α), so the state is unchanged when a feature shifts by 2π. Values differing by 2π are indistinguishable, and a feature ranging over [0,10π] wraps five times."}, {"title": "Encoding Data into Quantum States — Exercise 6", "chapter": "Encoding Data into Quantum States", "url": "02-data-encoding.html#exercise-6", "text": "Encode x=(3,4) and x=(6,8) by amplitude encoding. What is lost? Both give (0.6, 0.8): the norm is discarded, so positive rescalings produce the same state. If the norm carries information it must be encoded separately."}, {"title": "Encoding Data into Quantum States — Exercise 7", "chapter": "Encoding Data into Quantum States", "url": "02-data-encoding.html#exercise-7", "text": "Two encoded states have fidelity F = 0.5. What is the squared Euclidean distance between them, and what does that say about the encoding? ‖ψ−ψ'‖² = 2 − 1 = 1. For amplitude encoding this equals the squared distance between the corresponding normalised feature vectors, so the encoding preserves Euclidean geometry up to the norm."}, {"title": "Encoding Data into Quantum States — Exercise 8", "chapter": "Encoding Data into Quantum States", "url": "02-data-encoding.html#exercise-8", "text": "With L=3 encoding layers of a Pauli-generated map, list the accessible frequencies in one feature. The integers from −3 to 3: seven frequencies, of which ±1, ±2, ±3 correspond to three distinct periods. Adding layers increases the count linearly."}, {"title": "Encoding Data into Quantum States — Exercise 9", "chapter": "Encoding Data into Quantum States", "url": "02-data-encoding.html#exercise-9", "text": "A dataset has 1024 features per point. Compare the gate count of amplitude encoding with that of angle encoding at equal input size. Amplitude encoding uses 10 qubits and up to about 1024 two-qubit gates in the worst case; angle encoding uses 1024 qubits and 1024 rotations. Amplitude encoding is cheaper in qubits and comparable in gates here, but its worst-case preparation is exponential in qubits while its feature count is exponential too—so the crossover depends on the ratio of features to qubits."}, {"title": "Encoding Data into Quantum States — Exercise 10", "chapter": "Encoding Data into Quantum States", "url": "02-data-encoding.html#exercise-10", "text": "Choose an encoding for a task with 8 continuous features in [0,1] and 500 samples, and justify the choice in three sentences. Angle encoding with 8 qubits fits the data range inside one period, costs eight rotations per layer, and gives a kernel that is cheap to compute but classically simulable at this size; if a classically hard kernel is needed, add entangling layers and accept the deeper circuit; amplitude encoding would need only 3 qubits but its preparation cost dominates at 500 samples."}, {"title": "Three embeddings and what they preserve", "chapter": "Encoding Data into Quantum States", "url": "02-data-encoding.html#section-2-1", "text": "2.1 Three embeddings and what they preserve Three families cover almost everything in use. They differ in how many features a qubit can carry, in what they cost to prepare, and in which classical geometry survives the trip into Hilbert space. 2.1.1 Basis encoding The simplest embedding maps a bit string to a computational basis state: \\[x=(b_1,\\dots,b_n)\\in\\{0,1\\}^n\\ \\longmapsto\\ |x\\rangle=|b_1\\cdots b_n\\rangle .\\] It uses one qubit per bit and costs a layer of \\(X\\) gates. It is exact—measuring in the computational basis returns the input—and it preserves Hamming geometry: the distance between two encoded strings is the number of positions in which they differ. Its limitation is equally clear. Continuous features must be discretised first, and the discretisation is lossy in a way that is invisible to the model. More importantly, basis encoding gives no cheap way to evaluate a function of the whole string: a circuit that acts on \\(|x\\rangle\\) sees a single computational basis state, so any superposition that makes the computation parallel must be created independently of the data. Basis encoding is nevertheless the right choice for genuinely discrete problems—combinatorial inputs, Boolean formulas, or the oracle models used to prove query-complexity separations. It is the wrong choice when the data come from a continuous measurement. Worked example Encode the three-bit string \\(101\\) and the uniform superposition of all three-bit strings. How many gates does each require? Solution and reasoning The string needs \\(X\\) on qubits 1 and 3, giving \\(|101\\rangle\\): two gates. The uniform superposition is \\(H^{\\otimes3}|000\\rangle=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt8}\\sum_{x}|x\\rangle\\): three gates, and it is independent of any data. The contrast is the point. Data-dependent superpositions—the states that would let one circuit act on many inputs \"at once\"—are not produced by basis encoding; producing them requires a preparation whose cost scales with the number of amplitudes, which is the subject of Section 2.2. 2.1.2 Amplitude encoding Amplitude encoding stores a real vector in the amplitudes of a state: \\[x\\in\\mathbb R^{2^n}\\ \\longmapsto\\ |\\psi(x)\\rangle=\\frac{1}{\\|x\\|}\\sum_{i=0}^{2^n-1}x_i\\,|i\\rangle .\\] It is exponentially compressive: \\(n\\) qubits carry \\(2^n\\) numbers. It also preserves Euclidean geometry in a precise sense. For two unit vectors, the squared distance between the states is \\[\\bigl\\||\\psi(x)\\rangle-|\\psi(x')\\rangle\\bigr\\|^2=2-2\\langle\\psi(x)|\\psi(x')\\rangle,\\] so inner products of encoded states are inner products of normalised data vectors. A quantum computer that can estimate overlaps is therefore computing a linear kernel on the original features. Two costs come with the compression. The norm is discarded, and recovering it needs either a classical record or extra qubits. And preparing an arbitrary \\(2^n\\)-amplitude state requires \\(\\Theta(2^n)\\) gates in the worst case, because a general \\(n\\)-qubit state has \\(2^{n+1}-2\\) real parameters and each gate supplies only a constant number. The compression is real; the preparation is where the exponential reappears. Worked example Encode \\(x=(1,1,1,1)\\) and then \\(x=(1,2,3,4)\\) in two qubits. Which preparation is free? Solution and reasoning The first vector is already normalised up to a factor, giving \\(\\tfrac12(1,1,1,1)=H^{\\otimes2}|00\\rangle\\): two Hadamard gates, no data dependence beyond a check that the entries are equal. The second gives \\(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}}(1,2,3,4)\\), which is a generic two-qubit state with three independent parameters; it needs a general preparation—two or three two-qubit gates with computed angles. The example scales the wrong way for the optimistic reading: the \"free\" case is a special symmetric vector, and the generic case is the norm. An algorithm that assumes cheap amplitude preparation is assuming structure in the data, and that assumption belongs in the ledger. 2.1.3 Angle and feature-map encoding The third family uses features as rotation angles. With one feature per qubit, \\[x\\in\\mathbb R^{n}\\ \\longmapsto\\ |\\psi(x)\\rangle=\\bigotimes_{i=1}^{n}R_y(x_i)\\,|0\\rangle ,\\] which costs \\(n\\) gates for \\(n\\) features—linear, and cheap. The state is a product state, so it carries no entanglement; the model can still be nonlinear because the amplitudes are trigonometric functions of the data. Entangling gates interleaved with rotations turn this into a feature map . A single layer \\[U_\\phi(x)=U_{\\text{ent}}\\Bigl(\\bigotimes_i R_z(x_i)\\Bigr)H^{\\otimes n}\\] already produces states that are not product states, and the resulting kernel is no longer a product of univariate functions. Repeating the block \\(L\\) times creates frequencies up to \\(\\pm L\\) in each feature, which is the mechanism behind the Fourier series of Chapter 1. Worked example Compute the kernel of the product-state angle encoding for two features with \\(x=(0,0)\\) and \\(x'=(\\pi,0)\\). Solution and reasoning For a product state the overlap factorises: \\(\\langle\\psi(x)|\\psi(x')\\rangle=\\prod_i\\cos\\bigl((x_i-x'_i)/2\\bigr)\\). Here it is \\(\\cos(0)\\cos(\\pi/2)=0\\), so \\(K=0\\). The two points are orthogonal in Hilbert space even though their Euclidean distance is \\(\\pi\\). That is the encoding at work: the geometry of the features has been replaced by the geometry of rotations, and a distance of \\(\\pi\\) in one feature is the maximum separating distance. Two features differing by \\(2\\pi\\) would be indistinguishable—the trap examined in Section 2.2.3."}, {"title": "The cost of getting data in", "chapter": "Encoding Data into Quantum States", "url": "02-data-encoding.html#section-2-2", "text": "2.2 The cost of getting data in State preparation is the step most often assumed away. This section separates the cases where it is genuinely cheap from the cases where an oracle is being assumed, and then asks what the encoding does to distances. 2.2.1 Loading a vector: assumptions behind the oracle There are only four honest routes to a quantum state that encodes classical data: How a data state can be prepared, and what each route assumes. Route Cost Assumption The data are already quantum Sample supplied by the experiment No classical vector exists to load Structured preparation Polynomial in \\(n\\) The vector has structure—uniform, sparse, low rank, or generated by a known circuit Quantum memory (QRAM) Polylogarithmic queries A hardware device that returns amplitudes on demand, with its own error and scaling costs Generic preparation \\(\\Theta(2^n)\\) gates None; this is the honest baseline Most algorithms in the literature are stated with the third route. That is legitimate as complexity theory, because the oracle is part of the problem statement, but it is not a claim about hardware. When a paper says \"assume access to a quantum random-access memory\", the right response is to ask what the memory costs to build, how its errors propagate, and whether the claimed advantage survives when those costs are included. Worked example How many gates are needed to prepare an arbitrary 12-qubit state, and how many for the uniform superposition? Express both as a ratio. Solution and reasoning A general 12-qubit state has \\(2^{13}-2=8190\\) real parameters, so a gate-by-gate preparation needs on the order of \\(10^{3}\\) two-qubit gates. The uniform superposition needs 12 Hadamards. The ratio is roughly \\(700:1\\) at this size and doubles with every added qubit. This is why \"amplitude encoding of a \\(2^{12}\\)-dimensional feature vector\" is cheap only when the vector has a short description—a fact the encoding notation hides. 2.2.2 Normalisation and the geometry of encoded data Amplitude encoding maps a vector to a ray, so the encoding is invariant under positive rescaling: \\(x\\) and \\(cx\\) give the same state for \\(c>0\\). Two consequences follow. First, information is lost. If the norm carries signal—it often does, for example when it measures an intensity or a concentration—it must be encoded separately, in extra qubits or a classical side channel. Second, distances become angular. For unit vectors the state distance and the feature distance agree, but for vectors of different lengths the comparison is between directions only, and two points can be far apart in Euclidean terms while mapping to nearby states. Angle encoding has a different pathology: periodicity. Since \\(R_y(\\alpha+2\\pi)=-R_y(\\alpha)\\), the state is unchanged when a feature is shifted by \\(2\\pi\\). Feature values therefore have to be rescaled into an interval narrower than \\(2\\pi\\); if they are not, distinct data points receive identical states and no model can separate them. A rescaling that maps a large range into \\([0,2\\pi)\\) compresses the far end of the distribution, which is a modelling decision with consequences for the kernel. Worked example A feature ranges over \\([0,100]\\). What goes wrong with the naive map \\(x\\mapsto R_y(x)\\), and what is a reasonable repair? Solution and reasoning The map is \\(2\\pi\\)-periodic, so the range wraps about sixteen times: \\(x=0\\), \\(x=2\\pi\\), \\(x=4\\pi\\) and so on all give the same state, and points differing by about \\(6.28\\) become indistinguishable. A repair is an affine rescaling \\(x\\mapsto 2\\pi x/100\\), which makes the map injective on the range but compresses all separations by a factor of about \\(16\\). The compressed map is not wrong, but it changes the kernel: differences that were large in the data become small in Hilbert space. Recording the scale in the model specification is part of the specification, not an implementation detail. 2.2.3 What encoding does to distances Distances are the clearest way to compare encodings, because a kernel is a function of distance in feature space and the encoding changes what \"distance\" means. Kernel against feature difference for two encodings The angle-encoding kernel follows a squared cosine, falling to zero at a difference of pi and returning to one at two pi. A radial basis function kernel decays monotonically and stays near zero. The angle encoding therefore cannot distinguish features that differ by a full period. 1 0.5 0 0 π 2π 3π 4π difference in one feature angle encoding RBF kernel Kernel value against the difference in a single feature. The angle-encoding kernel \\(K=\\cos^2(\\Delta/2)\\) is periodic: it reaches zero at \\(\\Delta=\\pi\\) and returns to one at \\(\\Delta=2\\pi\\), so two features a full period apart are encoded identically. A radial basis function decays monotonically instead. Neither is better in general—but only one of them is injective on an unbounded range. This figure is the practical reason to state the encoding explicitly in every experiment. A model that appears to ignore a feature may simply be seeing the feature through a periodic map whose period is shorter than the data range, and a kernel that appears to separate classes may be relying on an aliasing artefact. Worked example For two points at feature difference \\(\\Delta\\), find the kernel value at \\(\\Delta=\\pi/2\\) and at \\(\\Delta=2\\pi\\), for the angle encoding and for an RBF kernel with bandwidth \\(\\sigma=1\\). Solution and reasoning Angle encoding: \\(K=\\cos^2(\\Delta/2)\\), so at \\(\\Delta=\\pi/2\\) we get \\(\\cos^2(\\pi/4)=1/2\\), and at \\(\\Delta=2\\pi\\) we get \\(\\cos^2(\\pi)=1\\). RBF: \\(\\exp(-\\Delta^2/2)\\), so at \\(\\Delta=\\pi/2\\) it is \\(\\exp(-\\pi^2/8)\\approx0.29\\), and at \\(2\\pi\\) it is \\(\\exp(-2\\pi^2)\\approx3\\times10^{-9}\\). The RBF says the far point is unrelated; the angle encoding says it is identical to the origin. Both are legitimate kernels, and the difference is entirely a property of the encoding."}, {"title": "Feature maps and kernels", "chapter": "Encoding Data into Quantum States", "url": "02-data-encoding.html#section-2-3", "text": "2.3 Feature maps and kernels An encoding plus an inner product is a kernel. Making that explicit turns encoding choices into statements about similarity, which is the language Chapter 3 uses to compare quantum and classical models on equal terms. 2.3.1 From embedding to kernel Given an encoding, define \\[K(x,x')=\\bigl|\\langle\\psi(x)|\\psi(x')\\rangle\\bigr|^2=\\bigl|\\langle 0|U_\\phi(x')^{\\dagger}U_\\phi(x)|0\\rangle\\bigr|^2 .\\] This is a positive semi-definite function of the pair, because it is a Gram matrix of state vectors, and it is computable on a quantum device by the overlap circuit of Section 3.2. For the product-state angle encoding it factorises: \\[K(x,x')=\\prod_{i=1}^{n}\\cos^2\\!\\Bigl(\\frac{x_i-x_i'}{2}\\Bigr),\\] which is a product of univariate functions—a classical kernel in disguise. This is worth internalising, because it explains a recurring result: circuits without entanglement, or with entanglement that only commutes with the encoding, produce kernels that a classical computer can evaluate in time linear in \\(n\\). Entanglement is necessary for the kernel to be hard to compute, though as Chapter 3 shows it is not sufficient for the kernel to be useful. Worked example Show that the product-state kernel equals one whenever \\(x_i-x_i'\\in2\\pi\\mathbb Z\\) for every feature, and interpret the result. Solution and reasoning Each factor is \\(\\cos^2(k\\pi)=1\\) for integer \\(k\\), so the product is one and the two points are indistinguishable to any model built on this kernel—regardless of how far apart they are in the original units. With more than one feature the ambiguity compounds: any vector difference whose components are multiples of \\(2\\pi\\) is invisible. This is an aliasing effect, not a limitation of quantum hardware, and it is removed only by choosing a different encoding or by rescaling the data so the range is smaller than the period. 2.3.2 The feature-map circuit The standard construction alternates a data-dependent rotation layer with a fixed entangling layer: \\[U_\\phi(x)=\\prod_{\\ell=1}^{L}\\Bigl[U_{\\text{ent}}\\prod_{i=1}^{n}R_z(x_i)\\Bigr]H^{\\otimes n}.\\] Each factor \\(R_z(x_i)\\) contributes a frequency of \\(1\\) in feature \\(i\\); the entangling layers mix features, so the composed map contains products such as \\(x_ix_j\\) inside phases. A widely used variant replaces the rotation layer with entangling rotations \\(\\exp(i x_i x_j Z_iZ_j)\\) for each pair, at a cost of \\(O(n^2)\\) gates per layer rather than \\(O(n)\\). The frequency content is what makes the map a model rather than a convenience. With \\(L\\) repetitions of a Pauli-generated encoding, the reachable functions are trigonometric polynomials whose spectra in each feature are contained in \\(\\{-L,\\dots,L\\}\\). More layers buy higher frequencies, and therefore the ability to fit more oscillatory targets—at the cost of more parameters and, as Chapter 6 shows, flatter gradients. Worked example How many distinct frequencies in a single feature are available with \\(L=2\\) encoding layers, and which ones? Solution and reasoning Each encoding layer contributes one unit of frequency in either direction, and the generator's eigenvalues are integers, so with two layers the accessible set is \\(\\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}\\): five frequencies, of which the symmetric pair \\(\\pm k\\) corresponds to the same period. A single layer would give only \\(\\{-1,0,1\\}\\). The count grows linearly in \\(L\\), which is why adding layers is the standard way to increase expressivity—and why the parameter count grows with it. 2.3.3 Choosing a map is modelling Because the map fixes the kernel, choosing it is choosing a prior about which points should be considered similar. Three consequences deserve to be stated plainly. Symmetries should match. If the task is invariant under a permutation of features, a map that treats features asymmetrically wastes capacity and data. Encoding a symmetry into the circuit is the quantum counterpart of using a convolutional architecture. Expressivity trades against concentration. A richer map produces states that are, on average, more nearly orthogonal—the kernel concentrates, and the model becomes insensitive to differences between points. This is the subject of Section 3.2.3 and the reason \"more expressivity\" is not an unconditional good. The map must be fixed before the test set is touched. Choosing the map, its depth, and its rescaling by observing test performance is fitting on the test set. The selection is part of the model and belongs to the training protocol. With these three commitments, the encoding is specified rather than inherited from a tutorial, and the comparisons of Chapter 3 become meaningful."}, {"title": "Quantum Kernels and Kernel Methods — Exercise 1", "chapter": "Quantum Kernels and Kernel Methods", "url": "03-kernels.html#exercise-1", "text": "State the representer theorem in one sentence. For a regularised empirical risk in a reproducing-kernel Hilbert space, a minimiser is a linear combination of kernel evaluations at the training points, f(x) = Σ α_i K(x, x_i), so the search is over N coefficients rather than a function space."}, {"title": "Quantum Kernels and Kernel Methods — Exercise 2", "chapter": "Quantum Kernels and Kernel Methods", "url": "03-kernels.html#exercise-2", "text": "Compute the target alignment of K = I with y = (1,1,1,−1). The inner product is 4, ‖I‖_F = 2, and ‖yyᵀ‖_F = 4, so the alignment is 4/(2·4) = 0.5."}, {"title": "Quantum Kernels and Kernel Methods — Exercise 3", "chapter": "Quantum Kernels and Kernel Methods", "url": "03-kernels.html#exercise-3", "text": "Is K = [[1,2],[2,1]] a valid kernel matrix? No. The eigenvalues are 3 and −1, so K is not positive semi-definite and the induced optimisation is not convex. A valid Gram matrix must have nonnegative eigenvalues."}, {"title": "Quantum Kernels and Kernel Methods — Exercise 4", "chapter": "Quantum Kernels and Kernel Methods", "url": "03-kernels.html#exercise-4", "text": "For K = I, λ = 1, and y = (1,−1), solve the ridge system for α. The matrix is 2I, so α = (0.5, −0.5). The prediction at a point with similarities (1,0) is 0.5."}, {"title": "Quantum Kernels and Kernel Methods — Exercise 5", "chapter": "Quantum Kernels and Kernel Methods", "url": "03-kernels.html#exercise-5", "text": "An entry with K = 0.5 is estimated from 100 shots. Give the standard deviation and the number of shots needed to halve it. Variance 0.25/100 = 0.0025, standard deviation 0.05. Halving it needs 400 shots, since the deviation scales as the inverse square root of the shot count."}, {"title": "Quantum Kernels and Kernel Methods — Exercise 6", "chapter": "Quantum Kernels and Kernel Methods", "url": "03-kernels.html#exercise-6", "text": "How many shots give a 10% relative error for a kernel value of 10⁻⁴? S ≈ 1/(0.01·10⁻⁴) = 10^5 shots for that single entry. Every entry of the Gram matrix pays this cost separately."}, {"title": "Quantum Kernels and Kernel Methods — Exercise 7", "chapter": "Quantum Kernels and Kernel Methods", "url": "03-kernels.html#exercise-7", "text": "Under the Haar concentration model with n = 8, give the mean and spread of an off-diagonal kernel entry. With d = 256 the mean is 1/256 ≈ 0.0039 and the spread is √(255/256³) ≈ 0.0039. The two are equal, so entries are noise around a constant."}, {"title": "Quantum Kernels and Kernel Methods — Exercise 8", "chapter": "Quantum Kernels and Kernel Methods", "url": "03-kernels.html#exercise-8", "text": "A Gram matrix has N = 1000 points and each entry needs 1000 shots. How many circuit executions, and how long at 1 microsecond per shot? 10^6 entries × 10^3 shots = 10^9 executions, about 1000 seconds. This excludes training, testing, and any repeated runs for error bars."}, {"title": "Quantum Kernels and Kernel Methods — Exercise 9", "chapter": "Quantum Kernels and Kernel Methods", "url": "03-kernels.html#exercise-9", "text": "Compute g(K1‖K2) and g(K2‖K1) for K1 = diag(1, 0.5) and K2 = I. For the first, K1⁻¹ = diag(1, 2), so the spectral norm is 2. For the second, √K1 K2⁻¹ √K1 = diag(1, 0.5), with norm 1. The quantity is asymmetric by construction."}, {"title": "Quantum Kernels and Kernel Methods — Exercise 10", "chapter": "Quantum Kernels and Kernel Methods", "url": "03-kernels.html#exercise-10", "text": "Design a three-step pre-flight check for a claimed quantum-kernel advantage on a new dataset. First, estimate a few thousand off-diagonal entries and check that the spread is small relative to the mean at the planned shot budget; second, fit the strongest classical kernel available under the same tuning budget; third, fix the feature map and hyperparameters using training folds only, then report accuracy differences with uncertainty over splits. A claim that survives all three is worth pursuing; one that fails the first is not."}, {"title": "Kernel methods without the quantum part", "chapter": "Quantum Kernels and Kernel Methods", "url": "03-kernels.html#section-3-1", "text": "3.1 Kernel methods without the quantum part A kernel encodes a notion of similarity, and the learning algorithm never touches the features themselves. Getting this structure right first makes the quantum addition easy to place. 3.1.1 The kernel trick and the representer theorem Let \\(K\\) be a positive semi-definite function on \\(\\mathcal X\\times\\mathcal X\\). It defines a reproducing-kernel Hilbert space \\(\\mathcal H_K\\) and a feature map \\(\\phi\\) with \\(K(x,x')=\\langle\\phi(x),\\phi(x')\\rangle\\), although the map never has to be written down. Consider any regularised empirical risk of the form \\[\\min_{f\\in\\mathcal H_K}\\ \\frac1N\\sum_{i=1}^N\\ell\\bigl(f(x_i),y_i\\bigr)+\\lambda\\|f\\|_{\\mathcal H_K}^2 .\\] The representer theorem states that a minimiser has the form \\[f(x)=\\sum_{i=1}^N\\alpha_i\\,K(x,x_i),\\] so the search is over \\(N\\) coefficients rather than over a function space. Substituting the expansion turns the problem into one that depends on the data only through the Gram matrix. That is the kernel trick: the cost scales with the number of examples, not with the dimension of the feature space—which is exactly why an exponentially large quantum feature space is not automatically disqualifying. Two consequences follow immediately. Training cost is at least \\(O(N^2)\\) to form \\(K\\) and \\(O(N^3)\\) to solve the resulting system, so the method is suited to moderate \\(N\\) with large feature dimension. And the whole model is determined by \\(K\\), so comparing two quantum models is comparing two matrices. Worked example Three training points have Gram matrix \\(K=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0.5&0\\\\0.5&1&0\\\\0&0&1\\end{pmatrix}\\) and dual coefficients \\(\\alpha=(1,-1,0.5)\\). Evaluate \\(f\\) at a test point whose similarities are \\(K(x,x_i)=(0.2,0.1,0.9)\\). Solution and reasoning The representer expansion gives \\(f(x)=1(0.2)+(-1)(0.1)+0.5(0.9)=0.2-0.1+0.45=0.55\\), with the sign of the prediction taken from the threshold of the classifier. The first two examples have opposite coefficients, so their contributions nearly cancel at a point where the two similarities are close. This is the mechanism by which a kernel classifier expresses \"different from this class but similar to that one\", and it is visible entirely in the row of the Gram matrix. 3.1.2 What a good kernel does A kernel is useful when its similarity agrees with the labels. Two quantities capture the agreement. The first is target alignment , the normalised inner product between \\(K\\) and the label kernel \\(yy^{\\top}\\): \\[A(K,y)=\\frac{\\langle K,\\,yy^{\\top}\\rangle}{\\|K\\|_F\\,\\|yy^{\\top}\\|_F}.\\] It is cheap to compute and useful for diagnostics, but it is not a performance guarantee—a kernel can align well with the training labels and still generalise poorly. The second is the eigenvalue spectrum. A kernel with a few large eigenvalues behaves like a low-dimensional linear model; a kernel with many comparable eigenvalues can fit anything and needs strong regularisation. The spectrum is where concentration damages a quantum kernel, as Section 3.2.3 shows. Two further properties are non-negotiable. \\(K\\) must be positive semi-definite, or the optimisation is not convex and the representer theorem does not apply; and the diagonal should be constant if the kernel is to be translation invariant—otherwise the model has a built-in preference for some training points. Worked example Compute the alignment of the identity kernel \\(K=I\\) with labels \\(y=(1,1,-1,-1)\\), and of the rank-one kernel \\(K=J\\) with the same labels. Solution and reasoning For \\(K=I\\): \\(\\langle I,yy^{\\top}\\rangle=\\sum_i y_i^2=4\\), \\(\\|I\\|_F=2\\), \\(\\|yy^{\\top}\\|_F=\\|y\\|^2=4\\), so \\(A=4/(2\\cdot4)=0.5\\). For \\(K=J\\): \\(\\langle J,yy^{\\top}\\rangle=(\\sum_i y_i)^2=0\\), so \\(A=0\\). The identity kernel declares every point unrelated to every other, and the constant kernel declares them all identical; neither can express the two-cluster structure. Alignment detects both failures without training anything. 3.1.3 Regularisation and the dual problem For squared loss, substituting the representer expansion gives the linear system \\[(K+\\lambda I)\\,\\alpha=y,\\qquad f(x)=\\sum_i\\alpha_i K(x,x_i).\\] The parameter \\(\\lambda\\) does two jobs at once: it controls overfitting, and it controls conditioning. The solution's sensitivity to noise in \\(K\\) is governed by the condition number \\(\\kappa(K+\\lambda I)\\), which grows as the smallest eigenvalue of \\(K\\) shrinks. Every kernel matrix estimated from finitely many shots is noisy, so an ill-conditioned \\(K\\) turns shot noise into large coefficient errors—a failure mode that has nothing to do with the quantum model's expressivity. For a soft-margin classifier the dual is a quadratic programme in \\(\\alpha\\) with box constraints, again depending on the data only through \\(K\\). In both cases the practical message is the same: the kernel matrix is the interface, and its numerical properties decide whether training succeeds. Worked example Let \\(K=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0.99\\\\0.99&1\\end{pmatrix}\\) and \\(y=(1,-1)\\). Solve the ridge system for \\(\\lambda=0.01\\) and for \\(\\lambda=1\\), and compare the coefficient magnitudes. Solution and reasoning The eigenvalues of \\(K\\) are \\(1.99\\) and \\(0.01\\). With \\(\\lambda=0.01\\) the shifted eigenvalues are \\(2.0\\) and \\(0.02\\), a condition number of \\(100\\); solving gives coefficients of roughly \\((0.5,-0.5)\\) amplified along the near-null direction, with magnitude about \\(25\\) in the eigenbasis. With \\(\\lambda=1\\) the eigenvalues become \\(2.99\\) and \\(1.01\\), the condition number falls to about \\(3\\), and the coefficients are order one. The data did not change; only the numerical conditioning did—which is why an experiment must report \\(\\lambda\\) alongside any accuracy figure."}, {"title": "Estimating a quantum kernel", "chapter": "Quantum Kernels and Kernel Methods", "url": "03-kernels.html#section-3-2", "text": "3.2 Estimating a quantum kernel A quantum feature map defines a kernel by an inner product of states. Estimating one entry is a small, well-understood circuit problem; estimating a matrix of them is where the cost appears. 3.2.1 Overlap as a kernel value With \\(U_\\phi(x)|0\\rangle=|\\psi(x)\\rangle\\), the kernel is the squared overlap \\[K(x,x')=\\bigl|\\langle 0|U_\\phi(x')^{\\dagger}U_\\phi(x)|0\\rangle\\bigr|^2 .\\] The estimate is direct: apply \\(U_\\phi(x)\\), then \\(U_\\phi(x')^{\\dagger}\\), and measure all qubits in the computational basis. The probability of the all-zeros outcome is exactly \\(K(x,x')\\), so if the outcome occurs \\(c\\) times in \\(S\\) shots, \\(\\hat K=c/S\\) is an unbiased estimate with variance \\(K(1-K)/S\\). No ancilla and no tomography are required—one circuit, run many times. This is the reason quantum kernels became a practical research topic: the entry cost is a single circuit whose depth is twice the encoding depth, not an exponential state-reconstruction procedure. The depth still matters, because near-term devices accumulate error per gate, and the circuit must be run once per matrix entry. Worked example An entry with true value \\(K=0.25\\) is estimated from \\(S=400\\) shots. Give the standard deviation, and the number of shots needed to halve it. Solution and reasoning The variance is \\(K(1-K)/S=0.25\\cdot0.75/400=4.7\\times10^{-4}\\), so the standard deviation is about \\(0.0217\\)—roughly 9% of the value. Halving that requires quadrupling the shots to 1600, because the deviation scales as \\(S^{-1/2}\\). The arithmetic is unforgiving: every factor of two in precision costs a factor of four in circuit executions, and the factor applies to every entry of the Gram matrix separately. 3.2.2 Shot noise and the cost of one entry Because \\(\\hat K\\) is a sample mean, its relative error is what matters for a classifier: \\[\\frac{\\sigma_{\\hat K}}{K}=\\sqrt{\\frac{1-K}{SK}}\\approx\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{SK}}\\quad (K\\ll1).\\] The approximation exposes the danger. If the typical kernel value is small—say \\(K\\sim2^{-n}\\), which is what concentration produces—then constant relative precision requires \\(S\\sim2^{n}\\) shots per entry . The Gram matrix then costs \\(\\Theta(N^2 2^n)\\) circuit executions, and the exponential that amplitude encoding avoided reappears in the estimator. Shots needed for a fixed relative precision The number of shots required for ten percent relative precision grows as one over the kernel value. For kernel values near a thousandth, about a hundred thousand shots are needed per matrix entry. 10⁶ 10³ 1 10⁻⁶ 10⁻⁴ 10⁻² 1 typical kernel value K K = 10⁻⁴ → 10⁵ shots Shots per matrix entry for a 10% relative error, from \\(S\\approx100/K\\). The relation is a straight line on log–log axes: every additional order of magnitude in smallness of the kernel costs an order of magnitude in shots. Concentration, which makes \\(K\\) small, therefore attacks the estimator as well as the model. Worked example For \\(n=10\\) qubits under the concentration model of Section 3.2.3, the typical off-diagonal entry is about \\(2^{-n}\\). How many shots give a 10% relative error? Solution and reasoning \\(K\\approx2^{-10}\\approx9.8\\times10^{-4}\\). A 10% relative error needs a standard deviation of \\(0.1K\\), so \\(K(1-K)/S=(0.1K)^2\\), giving \\(S\\approx(1-K)/(0.01K)\\approx10^{5}\\). Each Gram-matrix entry then needs about a hundred thousand shots; with \\(N=500\\) points the matrix has \\(2.5\\times10^{5}\\) entries, so the total is \\(2.5\\times10^{10}\\) circuit executions. The estimator, not the circuit, has become the bottleneck. 3.2.3 Concentration: kernels that go flat The final difficulty is structural. For a feature map whose states behave like random states in a \\(d=2^n\\)-dimensional space, the squared overlap between two distinct encoded points behaves like \\(X=\\bigl|\\langle\\psi|\\phi\\rangle\\bigr|^2\\) with \\(X\\sim\\text{Beta}(1,d-1)\\). Hence \\[\\mathbb E[X]=\\frac1d,\\qquad \\operatorname{Var}[X]=\\frac{d-1}{d^{3}}\\approx\\frac{1}{d^{2}},\\] so the typical off-diagonal entry is \\(1/d\\) and its spread is also about \\(1/d\\). Two conclusions follow, and they point in opposite directions. In absolute terms the matrix approaches a constant: entries shrink towards zero, so the kernel's ability to distinguish pairs by their similarity fades. In relative terms the noise does not shrink at all: the fluctuations are as large as the mean, so every entry is a poor estimate unless the shot count grows exponentially. This is the mechanism behind the exponential concentration results for quantum kernels. It is a statement about the model , not about hardware: a kernel whose entries all approach the same value can be reproduced by a trivial classifier, and no amount of shot precision restores the lost distinction. Explore kernel concentration Assume the encoded states behave like Haar-random states in dimension d = 2 n . The curve shows the standard deviation of an off-diagonal kernel entry against the number of qubits; the mean is 1/d. Qubits n: 6 Kernel spread against qubit count The spread of an off-diagonal kernel entry falls roughly by half for every added qubit, on a logarithmic axis. The current point and numeric output update with the control. 10⁰ 10⁻² 10⁻⁴ 10⁻⁶ 1 6 12 Dimension: 64 . Mean entry: 0.015625 . Spread: 0.015502 . Spread is comparable to the mean: entries are noise around a constant. Without JavaScript, the curve shows all twelve values and the numbers above are for n = 6. The spread halves with every added qubit, but so does the mean—so the ratio stays near one, and the kernel never becomes more informative with relative precision."}, {"title": "When a quantum kernel can help", "chapter": "Quantum Kernels and Kernel Methods", "url": "03-kernels.html#section-3-3", "text": "3.3 When a quantum kernel can help Concentration rules out one kind of hope—that a richer feature map automatically gives better similarity—but not every quantum kernel is concentrated. This section states the conditions under which a kernel can help and the checks that a serious comparison needs. 3.3.1 Geometric difference To compare two kernels on the same data, Huang and colleagues introduce a quantity that measures how differently they organise the feature space: \\[g(K_1\\|K_2)=\\Bigl\\|\\sqrt{K_2}\\,K_1^{-1}\\sqrt{K_2}\\Bigr\\|_\\infty ,\\] where \\(\\infty\\) is the spectral norm and the kernels are taken on the training set. A large \\(g\\) means the two kernels induce very different geometries. The relevant theorem, stated with its hypotheses, says roughly that if a quantum kernel can fit the target well, then either the classical kernel can too, or \\(g\\) is large; a provable advantage therefore requires both a large geometric difference and a target that the quantum geometry happens to match. This is a useful discipline. It converts \"our kernel is more expressive\" into two checkable numbers: the geometric difference, and the alignment on training folds. Neither is decisive on its own, but a claimed advantage with a small \\(g\\) is in immediate trouble. Worked example Take \\(K_1=\\operatorname{diag}(1,0.1)\\) and \\(K_2=I\\) on two training points. Compute \\(g(K_1\\|K_2)\\) and \\(g(K_2\\|K_1)\\). Solution and reasoning For the first, \\(\\sqrt{K_2}K_1^{-1}\\sqrt{K_2}=\\operatorname{diag}(1,10)\\), whose spectral norm is \\(10\\). For the second, \\(\\sqrt{K_1}K_2^{-1}\\sqrt{K_1}=\\operatorname{diag}(1,0.1)\\), with norm \\(1\\). So \\(g\\) is not symmetric: \\(g(K_1\\|K_2)=10\\) but \\(g(K_2\\|K_1)=1\\). The asymmetry is the point—the expression measures how far \\(K_1\\) stretches the geometry that \\(K_2\\) defines, and reversing the roles is a different question. Numerical care is needed too: with a near-singular \\(K_1\\) the inverse amplifies noise, which is why implementations regularise before inverting. 3.3.2 Constructed data and the extrapolation question The best-known separation result for quantum kernels uses data whose labels are defined by a discrete logarithm, together with a feature map built from the same structure. For that construction, the quantum kernel provably generalises from a polynomial number of examples while any classical kernel needs exponentially many. The result is real and worth understanding, and it is also the clearest example of why the three claim types of Chapter 1 matter. It establishes an existence separation: there is a task, a distribution, and a model with a stated access model that separates the two. It does not establish that the same separation survives on data anyone actually has, because the construction encodes the problem's structure into the feature map by hand—and if that structure is known, a classical algorithm can often be built from it too. The honest reading is that separations tell us where to look. They identify the structural conditions—hard-to-compute inner products, a target aligned with them, no classical shortcut—that a practical demonstration would have to satisfy. Worked example A paper reports a quantum kernel with 99% accuracy and a classical baseline with 60%. List three questions that must be answered before this counts as evidence of a separation. Solution and reasoning First, what is the access model? If the quantum feature map uses structure that a classical algorithm could also exploit, the comparison is between a clever model and a naive one. Second, how was the baseline tuned, and was it given the same tuning budget as the quantum model? Third, how large is the geometric difference, and does the advantage persist when the classical model is allowed the same number of features? A separation claim needs all three; a single accuracy number answers none of them. 3.3.3 A checklist before training The chapter's content condenses into a short protocol. Running it before a training run saves weeks. A pre-flight checklist for a quantum kernel experiment. Check Why it matters Is the encoding injective on the data range? Periodic maps make distant points identical (Section 2.2.3) Is the circuit classically simulable at this size? If so, the classical simulator is the baseline What is the typical off-diagonal entry, and its spread? Concentration makes the classifier blind (Section 3.2.3) How many shots per entry, and what is the resulting matrix error? Shot noise propagates through \\(K^{-1}\\) What is the condition number of \\(K+\\lambda I\\)? Ill-conditioning amplifies that noise What is the geometric difference from the classical kernel? A small value leaves no room for an advantage Was the map fixed before the test set was opened? Otherwise the comparison is leaking (Chapter 1) Chapter 4 turns to the other major family—variational models trained by a classical optimiser—and Chapter 6 shows that the trainability problems there are cousins of the concentration effect derived here."}, {"title": "Variational Quantum Models — Exercise 1", "chapter": "Variational Quantum Models", "url": "04-variational.html#exercise-1", "text": "Name the four choices that define a variational model, and say which one is usually called the ansatz. The data-encoding map, the parameterised circuit, the readout observables, and the training rule. Only the parameterised circuit is normally called the ansatz, yet all four determine what the model can represent, and the readout alone can decide whether it trains."}, {"title": "Variational Quantum Models — Exercise 2", "chapter": "Variational Quantum Models", "url": "04-variational.html#exercise-2", "text": "A six-qubit hardware-efficient layer applies R_y and R_z to every qubit, then a ring of six CNOTs. Count the parameters and CNOTs for two layers. Each layer has 2 x 6 = 12 angles and 6 CNOTs, so p = 24 and 12 CNOTs. Compare with the 2^7 - 2 = 126 real parameters of a general six-qubit pure state: the model is a 24-dimensional slice."}, {"title": "Variational Quantum Models — Exercise 3", "chapter": "Variational Quantum Models", "url": "04-variational.html#exercise-3", "text": "An eight-qubit circuit is cut into two groups of four. What is the largest Schmidt rank a two-layer ansatz can produce across that cut, and what does a maximally entangled state need? Two layers give rank at most 2^2 = 4, while a maximally entangled 4|4 state needs rank 2^4 = 16. No training schedule can close that gap; the ansatz, not the optimiser, sets the floor."}, {"title": "Variational Quantum Models — Exercise 4", "chapter": "Variational Quantum Models", "url": "04-variational.html#exercise-4", "text": "Counts from 1,000 shots of a two-qubit experiment are 450 for 00, 50 for 01, 60 for 10, and 440 for 11. Estimate the expectation of Z on the first qubit and of Z (x) Z. For Z on qubit 1 the sign is + for 00 and 01, so (450 + 50 - 60 - 440)/1000 = 0. For Z (x) Z the sign is + only for 00 and 11, giving (450 + 440 - 50 - 60)/1000 = 0.78. The two marginals are balanced while the correlation is strong, which a single-qubit readout would have missed entirely."}, {"title": "Variational Quantum Models — Exercise 5", "chapter": "Variational Quantum Models", "url": "04-variational.html#exercise-5", "text": "How many shots are needed to resolve a single Pauli expectation value to plus or minus 0.005? S = 1/0.005^2 = 40,000 shots, and that is for one term only. Three decimal digits on a full Hamiltonian is a million-shot-per-term proposition unless the allocation is optimised."}, {"title": "Variational Quantum Models — Exercise 6", "chapter": "Variational Quantum Models", "url": "04-variational.html#exercise-6", "text": "Two terms have coefficients 0.5 and 0.3 with 1,000 and 5,000 shots. Estimate the standard deviation of the combined estimator, then propose a better allocation for a fixed total of 6,000 shots. The variances are 0.25/1000 = 2.5e-4 and 0.09/5000 = 1.8e-5, summing to 2.68e-4, so the deviation is about 0.016. Allocating shots as |c_j| splits 6,000 into 3,750 and 2,250, giving 0.25/3750 + 0.09/2250 = 1.07e-4, a deviation of about 0.010 with the same budget."}, {"title": "Variational Quantum Models — Exercise 7", "chapter": "Variational Quantum Models", "url": "04-variational.html#exercise-7", "text": "A qubit has symmetric readout error 0.08 and the measured excited-state population is 0.60. Correct it and give the variance penalty. The corrected value is (0.60 - 0.08)/0.84 = 0.619, so the raw number was biased low by about 0.019. The variance is inflated by 1/(0.84)^2 = 1.42, so about 42% more shots are needed. At 15% error the factor exceeds two and the inversion becomes unreliable."}, {"title": "Variational Quantum Models — Exercise 8", "chapter": "Variational Quantum Models", "url": "04-variational.html#exercise-8", "text": "An optimiser needs 60 measurement settings for a 200-term Hamiltonian, 2,000 shots per setting, and 150 iterations. Count the shots and the time at one microsecond each. 60 x 2,000 x 150 = 1.8 x 10^7 shots, about 18 seconds of pure shot time. Wall-clock time on hardware is dominated by latency and reset, so a run of this size takes hours, not seconds; and a gradient-based optimiser multiplies the per-iteration cost again."}, {"title": "Variational Quantum Models — Exercise 9", "chapter": "Variational Quantum Models", "url": "04-variational.html#exercise-9", "text": "Adding layers makes a variational model better, because more depth means more expressibility. No. Depth raises expressibility and, past a point, destroys trainability: gradients concentrate and hardware error accumulates with every layer. The useful depth is the smallest one whose reachable set contains a good solution, which is why adaptive schemes add layers only when the loss stops improving."}, {"title": "Variational Quantum Models — Exercise 10", "chapter": "Variational Quantum Models", "url": "04-variational.html#exercise-10", "text": "Design a measurement plan for the four-qubit Heisenberg Hamiltonian with six XX+YY+ZZ bond terms plus four field terms, and state how many settings you need. ZZ on the four bonds plus the four Z field terms are all qubit-wise commuting, so they share one setting. XX terms on different bonds overlap, so a chain needs two alternating settings for the XX strings and two for YY; with the field terms folded in, five to seven settings is realistic. Estimating each setting to plus or minus 0.01 costs 10^4 shots each, so one energy evaluation is roughly 5 x 10^4 to 7 x 10^4 shots before any optimisation."}, {"title": "Building a variational ansatz", "chapter": "Variational Quantum Models", "url": "04-variational.html#section-4-1", "text": "4.1 Building a variational ansatz An ansatz is a circuit family with free parameters. The name suggests a guess, and that is accurate: every structural decision—which gates, which connectivity, how deep—is a prior about the solution, made before any data is seen. 4.1.1 The four choices that define a model A variational model is fixed by four decisions, and confusing them is a common source of muddled comparisons. The first is the data-encoding map \\(x\\mapsto\\rho(x)\\) or \\(x\\mapsto|\\psi(x)\\rangle\\), which Chapter 2 analysed. The second is the ansatz \\(U(\\mathbf\\theta)\\), the parameterised circuit applied after—or interleaved with—the encoding. The third is the readout : which observables are measured and how their expectation values are combined into a scalar. The fourth is the training rule : the loss, the optimiser, and the stopping condition. Only the second is usually called \"the ansatz\", yet all four determine what the model can represent, and the readout alone can change a trainable model into an untrainable one (Section 4.3.2). The output of the model is an expectation value, \\[\\hat y(x;\\mathbf\\theta)=\\langle 0|U^{\\dagger}(\\mathbf\\theta)\\,U^{\\dagger}_{\\phi}(x)\\,O\\,U_{\\phi}(x)\\,U(\\mathbf\\theta)|0\\rangle ,\\] where \\(O\\) is a Hermitian observable. Since \\(\\mathbf\\theta\\) enters through unitary gates, \\(\\hat y\\) is a smooth, bounded, periodic function of each angle: a trigonometric polynomial. That single fact explains both the smoothness that optimisers exploit and the redundancies that make parameter space larger than it needs to be. Worked example A hardware-efficient layer on \\(n=10\\) qubits applies \\(R_y\\) and \\(R_z\\) to every qubit, then a ladder of \\(n-1\\) nearest-neighbour CNOTs. Count the parameters and two-qubit gates for \\(L=3\\) layers. Solution and reasoning Each layer carries \\(2n=20\\) angles and \\(n-1=9\\) CNOTs, so three layers give \\(p=60\\) parameters and 27 CNOTs. Compare that with the \\(2^{11}-2=2046\\) real parameters of a general 10-qubit pure state: the ansatz explores a 60-dimensional slice of a 2046-dimensional manifold. That ratio is the useful statement of expressivity, and it is why \"the state space is exponentially large\" says nothing about the model. It also sets the scale for generalisation questions in Chapter 7: 60 parameters against a few hundred samples is a very different statistical situation from 2046. 4.1.2 Hardware-efficient and problem-inspired circuits Two families dominate practice, and they sit at opposite ends of a trade-off between what the hardware likes and what the problem knows. Hardware-efficient ansätze use gates that the device executes natively—single-qubit rotations and the entanglers available on its connectivity graph—arranged in repeating layers. They are shallow, calibration-friendly, and problem-agnostic: nothing about the circuit knows whether the task is chemistry, classification, or optimisation. Their weakness is that being agnostic they must be expressive, and expressiveness is exactly what Section 4.1.3 and Chapter 6 identify as dangerous. Problem-inspired ansätze borrow structure. The Hamiltonian variational ansatz and the unitary coupled-cluster family build layers from the terms of the problem Hamiltonian, so the circuit conserves particle number and symmetry by construction; QAOA alternates a problem phase separator with a mixer. These circuits are far less expressive, and that is the point: if the target state lies in the family, restricting the search is a gain, not a loss. The risk moves elsewhere—an over-restricted ansatz can miss the ground state entirely, and the residual error looks like an optimisation failure when it is a modelling one. Four ansatz families and the properties that decide where each belongs. Family Built from Expressibility Entanglement Main risk Fits Hardware-efficient Native rotations plus device entanglers High; rises quickly with depth Tuned by the entangler pattern Concentration and plateaus Near-term devices, generic tasks Hamiltonian variational Terms of the problem Hamiltonian Restricted by construction Follows the interaction graph Cannot reach states outside the family Ground-state problems with known structure Unitary coupled cluster Fermionic excitations Restricted; symmetry-preserving Grows with excitation rank Circuit depth from Trotterisation Molecular energies Data re-uploading Repeated encoding interleaved with rotations High in the number of uploads Optional Encoding cost; periodicity (Chapter 2) Small classification tasks Worked example A molecular Hamiltonian ansatz uses one layer per Hamiltonian term, each term being a Trotterised excitation with depth 6. The Hamiltonian has 84 terms. Estimate the circuit depth, and say what happens to accuracy if the depth budget is 200. Solution and reasoning One layer costs about 6 gates deep and there are 84 terms, so a single sweep needs roughly 500 operations—already more than twice the stated budget. With a budget of 200 the experimenter must either truncate the term list (losing the correlation energy those terms carry) or use a shallower Trotter step. Both are legitimate, but each must be reported, because a variational energy computed from a truncated ansatz is an upper bound for a different Hamiltonian than the one in the abstract. This is the practical content of \"problem-inspired\": you inherit the physics and its cost at the same time. 4.1.3 Depth, entanglement, and what a shallow circuit cannot do Depth is not a free resource, and it has a precise consequence for entanglement that is worth stating as a bound rather than a worry. Cut a circuit's qubits into two groups. Every entangling gate straddling the cut is a two-qubit gate, and a two-qubit gate can at most double the Schmidt rank across that cut. A circuit of depth \\(L\\)—that is, \\(L\\) layers, each acting on all qubits—therefore produces a state whose Schmidt rank across any contiguous cut is at most \\(2^{L}\\). For \\(n=10\\) qubits cut into \\(5+5\\), a maximally entangled state needs rank \\(2^{5}=32\\), so \\(L=3\\) layers can supply at most rank 8. If the target state requires more, the ansatz has a floor in its loss that no amount of training removes. This is a statement about what shallow circuits cannot do, and it is easy to over-read. Most learning targets are not maximally entangled; a classical model's target function is usually low-complexity, and the corresponding quantum state may be reachable at modest depth. The useful discipline is to ask, for a given target, which side of the bound you are on, and to test it by measuring the rank or the entanglement entropy of the trained state. Expressibility is measured rather than assumed. The Meyer–Wallach measure \\[Q(|\\psi\\rangle)=1-\\frac1n\\sum_{k=1}^{n}\\operatorname{tr}\\bigl(\\rho_k^2\\bigr)\\] is zero for product states and grows as the state's local entanglement grows, and the frame potential or a divergence from the Haar measure compares an ansatz's output distribution with the uniform one. The empirical regularity, documented across many ansatz families, is that expressibility and trainability pull in opposite directions: the circuits expressive enough to contain the target are often the ones whose gradients vanish. Chapter 6 quantifies that. Worked example Compute the Meyer–Wallach measure for \\(|000\\rangle\\) and for the three-qubit GHZ state \\((|000\\rangle+|111\\rangle)/\\sqrt2\\). Solution and reasoning For \\(|000\\rangle\\) every single-qubit reduced state is pure, so \\(\\operatorname{tr}(\\rho_k^2)=1\\) and \\(Q=0\\). For GHZ every single-qubit reduced state is maximally mixed, \\(\\operatorname{tr}(\\rho_k^2)=1/2\\), so \\(Q=1-\\tfrac13\\cdot\\tfrac32=1/2\\). A non-zero \\(Q\\) therefore certifies genuine multipartite entanglement—but \\(Q\\) cannot distinguish a state that is useful for a task from one that is merely entangled, and it takes no account of the circuit cost of preparing the state. It is a diagnostic of the ansatz, not an objective."}, {"title": "From expectation value to loss", "chapter": "Variational Quantum Models", "url": "04-variational.html#section-4-2", "text": "4.2 From expectation value to loss A circuit on its own produces nothing usable. Between the unitary and the optimiser sit three steps with their own costs: decomposing the observable, choosing measurement settings, and paying for statistical precision. 4.2.1 Pauli decomposition and measurement settings Almost every observable of interest is a sum of Pauli strings, \\[O=\\sum_{j=1}^{m}c_j P_j ,\\qquad \\langle O\\rangle=\\sum_{j=1}^{m}c_j\\langle P_j\\rangle .\\] Each \\(P_j\\) has to be estimated, and a single circuit run yields one bit-string, so it estimates many \\(P_j\\) at once only if they can be measured in the same basis. Two Pauli strings are qubit-wise commuting when, on every qubit, they either share the same Pauli or one of them is the identity; such a set can be read out from a single setting by rotating each qubit into the right basis. Strings that commute but are not qubit-wise commuting can also be grouped, at the price of extra basis-changing gates inside the circuit. The grouping problem is a graph-colouring problem, and it is worth solving before writing any code, because the number of settings multiplies every subsequent cost. Eight Pauli terms grouped into three measurement settings Three rows labelled setting one to three. Setting one holds six all-Z terms, setting two holds the XX term, and setting three holds the YY term. setting 1 setting 2 setting 3 ZIII IZII IIZI IIIZ ZZII ZIZI XXII YYII six terms, one readout basis: Z on every qubit X on qubits 1 and 2 Y on qubits 1 and 2 Grouping eight Pauli terms into measurement settings. All six all-Z strings share one basis. The \\(XXII\\) and \\(YYII\\) strings commute with nothing in that group and anticommute with each other, so each needs its own setting: eight terms become three circuit runs per iteration, not eight. Worked example An operator on four qubits has the eight terms grouped in Figure 4-2 with coefficients \\(0.5,0.5,-0.2,-0.2,0.3,0.1,0.15,0.15\\). Estimate how many settings are needed and how they are measured. Solution and reasoning Three settings suffice. The six \\(Z\\)-type strings are measured together after no basis change. \\(XXII\\) requires a Hadamard on qubits 1 and 2 before readout and \\(YYII\\) requires the \\(Y\\)-basis rotation \\(S^{\\dagger}H\\) on both. Note that a commutation-based grouping would do no better here: \\(XXII\\) and \\(YYII\\) anticommute on qubits 1 and 2. In larger Hamiltonians the gain from grouping is substantial—typically an order of magnitude in the number of settings—which is why measurement planning is a first-class part of the compilation problem rather than an afterthought. 4.2.2 Shot noise and the precision target Every expectation value is an estimate. For a Pauli string \\(P\\) measured in \\(S\\) shots, the estimator \\(\\hat{\\langle P\\rangle}\\) has variance \\[\\operatorname{Var}\\bigl(\\hat{\\langle P\\rangle}\\bigr)=\\frac{1-\\langle P\\rangle^2}{S}\\le\\frac1S ,\\] so its standard deviation is at worst \\(1/\\sqrt S\\). To resolve a quantity to \\(\\pm\\epsilon\\) you need \\(S\\approx1/\\epsilon^2\\) shots: two decimal digits cost ten thousand shots, three digits a million. Because the terms combine, the error on the total is \\[\\sigma_O=\\sqrt{\\sum_{j=1}^{m}\\frac{c_j^2\\,(1-\\langle P_j\\rangle^2)}{S_j}}\\ ,\\] which tells you where to spend: terms with large coefficients deserve most of the shots, and this allocation problem has a closed-form solution when the variances are known or can be estimated from a pilot run. The consequence for training is severe and easy to forget. A gradient or a finite difference is a difference of expectation values, so its signal is the small quantity while the noise is the same large quantity. If the loss is measured to \\(\\pm0.01\\) and the gradient signal is \\(0.005\\), the optimiser is following noise. Precision requirements therefore have to be set per training run, and they grow with the number of parameters and iterations. Worked example A Hamiltonian estimator is built from the eight terms above with \\(S_j=2000\\) shots each, and all expectation values are taken to be zero for a worst-case bound. Estimate the standard deviation of the energy. Solution and reasoning The coefficients squared sum to \\(0.25+0.25+0.04+0.04+0.09+0.01+0.0225+0.0225=0.7175\\). Dividing by 2000 gives \\(3.59\\times10^{-4}\\), so \\(\\sigma_O\\approx0.019\\). A chemical accuracy target of \\(1.6\\times10^{-3}\\) hartree then needs roughly \\((0.019/0.0016)^2\\approx140\\) times more shots for the same allocation, and much of that has to be repeated at every optimisation step. This is why variational chemistry proposals quote shot budgets explicitly: an energy curve with \\(10^{-3}\\) error bars is a different claim from one with \\(2\\times10^{-2}\\) error bars. 4.2.3 Readout error and calibration Measurement is not ideal. A qubit read out as 1 was sometimes 0, and the effect is systematic rather than random. For a single qubit with symmetric flip probability \\(\\varepsilon\\), the observed and true distributions are related by an assignment matrix, \\[\\begin{pmatrix}p^{\\text{obs}}_{0}\\\\ p^{\\text{obs}}_{1}\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}1-\\varepsilon&\\varepsilon\\\\ \\varepsilon&1-\\varepsilon\\end{pmatrix}\\begin{pmatrix}p^{\\text{true}}_{0}\\\\ p^{\\text{true}}_{1}\\end{pmatrix},\\] which can be estimated by preparing every computational basis state and inverting. Two cautions follow. The inversion amplifies noise by \\(1/(1-2\\varepsilon)^2\\), so 3% readout error costs about 13% more shots—small—but 15% error costs more than twice as many, and the matrix becomes ill-conditioned. For \\(n\\) qubits the full matrix has \\(4^{n}\\) entries, which is why practitioners use a tensored or correlated model fitted to a limited set of calibration circuits, accepting residual bias for tractability. The same logic governs gate error, but there the mitigation is different: errors accumulate with depth, so a deeper ansatz needs either error mitigation, which trades shots for accuracy, or error correction, which trades qubits. Both change the accounting, and neither is free. Worked example A qubit has symmetric readout error \\(\\varepsilon=0.03\\) and the measured excited-state population is \\(0.55\\). Correct it, and state the cost on the estimator's variance. Solution and reasoning Inverting gives \\(p^{\\text{true}}=(0.55-0.03)/0.94=0.5521\\), so the raw number was biased low by about 0.002. The variance inflation is \\(1/(1-2\\varepsilon)^2=1/0.8836\\approx1.13\\), so 13% more shots are needed for the same precision. Both effects are small here, and the example is included precisely to make the point that readout mitigation is a correction with a measurable price, not a free improvement—and to give you the arithmetic to check a claimed improvement rather than accepting it."}, {"title": "The optimisation problem in parameter space", "chapter": "Variational Quantum Models", "url": "04-variational.html#section-4-3", "text": "4.3 The optimisation problem in parameter space Training means minimising a loss over the \\(p\\) angles. Everything depends on the shape of that function, and the shape is set by choices made in Section 4.1—before any data arrived. 4.3.1 The landscape is a function of the circuit Write the empirical loss as \\(L(\\mathbf\\theta)=\\frac1N\\sum_i\\ell\\bigl(\\hat y(x_i;\\mathbf\\theta),y_i\\bigr)\\). It is smooth, because each \\(\\hat y\\) is a trigonometric polynomial in the angles. It is bounded. It is not convex in general, and cannot be made so without giving up the model. Two-parameter slice of a variational loss landscape A curve with a shallow local minimum on the left and a deeper global minimum on the right, with the initial point marked on the way down into the shallow minimum. local minimum global minimum initial point loss one direction in parameter space A one-dimensional slice through a two-parameter landscape, with the initial point on the slope of the shallower well. Gradient descent from here finds the local minimum and reports a converged loss. The reported value is an upper bound on the best available loss, and the gap between the two minima is what an unlucky initialisation costs. Two structural features are worth naming. First, parameter redundancy: because the circuit is periodic in each angle, distinct parameter vectors produce identical states, so the landscape has flat directions and the Hessian is singular. Second, the map from parameters to loss can be highly curved in some directions and almost flat in others—an ill-conditioned landscape that a first-order optimiser will crawl along. Both are properties of the ansatz, and both are visible before training by examining the spectrum of the classical Fisher information or the Hessian at a few random points. The practical point is that \"the optimiser failed\" is a diagnosis of last resort. It is usually a statement about the landscape, and the landscape is a design decision. 4.3.2 Global versus local cost functions How the measured terms are combined into one number changes trainability, not just scale. A global cost takes the whole observable at once, for instance \\(L=1-\\langle H\\rangle/\\|H\\|_{\\infty}\\), where \\(H\\) may have exponentially many terms. A local cost averages individual low-weight terms, \\(L=1-\\frac1m\\sum_j\\langle P_j\\rangle\\) with \\(m\\) terms of bounded weight. The difference is measurable. For global costs, the variance of the gradient at random initialisation decays exponentially in the number of qubits even at shallow depth; for local costs of bounded weight the decay is polynomial at shallow depth, becoming exponential only as depth grows. This is the cost-function-dependent barren-plateau result of Cerezo and colleagues (2021), and it is one of the few pieces of variational-circuit theory with direct design consequences: define the loss over local terms and combine the results classically. Worked example A gradient component decays like \\(2^{-n}\\) while the shot noise on its estimate stays fixed. How many more shots are needed at \\(n=10\\) to keep the same relative precision as at \\(n=1\\)? Solution and reasoning Relative precision requires \\(S\\propto 1/g^2\\) for a signal \\(g\\), so a signal smaller by \\(2^{-n}\\) costs \\(4^{n}\\) times more shots. At \\(n=10\\) that is \\(4^{10}\\approx1.05\\times10^{6}\\)—a million times the shots for the same relative accuracy. Note the trap in the word \"relative\": if the noise decayed as fast as the signal, the shot count would not change at all, and a plot of signal-to-noise would look flat while both quantities vanished. Always ask which of the two is decaying. 4.3.3 Regularisation, early stopping, and the prior you did not choose A variational model with \\(p\\) angles fitted to \\(N\\) samples will interpolate whenever \\(p\\gtrsim N\\), and interpolation is not generalisation. Three regularisers are available, and only one of them is usually discussed. Early stopping is the cheapest: monitor a validation set and stop when its loss turns upward. Since the parameters move slowly near flat regions, the number of iterations acts as an implicit constraint on the distance travelled from initialisation, and that constraint is a real prior. Explicit penalties —adding \\(\\lambda\\|\\mathbf\\theta\\|^2\\) to the loss, or constraining the parameter range—make the prior visible and tunable. The third and least discussed is the ansatz itself : choosing a circuit that conserves particle number, or that only entangles nearest neighbours, has already excluded most functions, and that exclusion is worth more than any amount of tuning in the small-sample regime. The consequence for reporting is blunt. Two papers comparing quantum and classical models with different inductive biases are comparing the biases, not the hardware. State the ansatz, the parameter count, the regularisation, and the stopping rule, or the comparison cannot be interpreted. Chapter 7 develops the statistical side of this, and Chapter 8 turns it into a checklist for advantage claims. Cost accounting for one training run of the four-qubit example above. Stage Settings Shots per setting Repetitions Total shots Readout calibration 16 basis states 5,000 1 80,000 Optimisation loop 3 2,000 150 iterations 900,000 Final evaluation 3 20,000 1 60,000 Total 1,040,000 About a million shots, or roughly one second of pure shot time at a microsecond per shot—and that number is misleading in the direction people least expect. On real hardware the wall-clock cost is dominated by latency, reset, and compilation, so a million shots may take hours. The arithmetic above is a lower bound on cost and an upper bound on optimism."}];
